        "thinking": "'t have a complete understanding of the category of motives itself, and the con"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "jectures about motivic Galois groups (like the Hodge and Tate conjectures) remain among the deepest open problems in arithmetic"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " geometry"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " The"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " category of pure motives depends on which equivalence relation we use for cycles, while mixed motives extend this to general varieties though"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " they're still not fully formalized—Voevodsky's triangulated category is a working"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " approximation. Through Tannakian formalism, we can recover a pro-algebraic group (the motiv"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "ic Galois group) from the category of motives and its forgetful functor to vector spaces, which generalizes how"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " any group is determined by its representations."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nIf"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " we could construct the abelian category of mixed motives properly, the various cohomology theories—"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "Betti, de Rham, étale—would correspond to different fiber functors into this group, and the comparison isomorphisms between them would reflect"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " the group's torsor structure. The main obstacles are actually constructing this category and resol"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "ving the standard conjectures, particularly whether numerical and homological equivalence coincide, along with Grothendieck's period conjecture about relating"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " different cohomological invariants."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nI"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " know that Jannsen proved the Tannakian property"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " for pure motives under the standard conjectures, Nori gave an unconditional construction of mixed motives, and the mixed Tate case"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " is relatively well-understood through polylogarithms and multiple zeta values. Now I should organize this pedagog"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "ically in French, building from the foundations up."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nI"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "'m structuring the response to start with classical Galois theory as"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " a paradigm, then move through multiple cohomologies, the concept of motives, Tannakian formalism, motivic Galois groups, and finally the open"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " problems. For the Tannakian part, I need to explain that an abelian rigid tensor category with"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " a faithful exact tensor functor to vector spaces is Tannakian, which means it's equivalent to the representation category of its"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " automorphism group."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nNow"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " I'm working through the theory of pure motives—starting with smooth projective varieties and algebraic cycles modulo an equivalence relation, where objects"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " are triples consisting of a variety, an idempotent correspondence, and a Tate twist. The category is linear over the"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " rationals and becomes abelian and semisimple when we use numerical equivalence. For"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " the motivic Galois group, assuming the standard conjectures hold, the category of numerically equivalent motives is semisimple,"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " which gives us a well-defined Galois group acting on it."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nThe Betti realization fun"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "ctor provides a fiber functor over the rationals, so the motivic Galois group becomes a pro-reductive group. When extending to"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " mixed motives, we'd allow non-smooth and non-proper varieties with weight and Hodge filtrations, though the resulting"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " Galois group would be pro-algebraic rather than reductive. The periods arise from comparing the Betti and"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " de Rham realizations, which relates back to how the motivic Galois group acts on these"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " different cohomology theories."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nGrothendieck's period conjecture"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " connects this to transcendence: the algebraic relations among periods should correspond"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " exactly to the dimension of the motivic Galois group, meaning all period relations come from the"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " underlying geometry. I should also bring in the Mumford-Tate group as the"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " Hodge-theoretic version of the motivic Galois group, and consider the ℓ-adic"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " Galois representations alongside the Betti picture."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nFor"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " a motive over ℚ, I have three key objects: the"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " Mumford-Tate group acting on the Betti realization with its Hodge structure, the algebraic monodromy group from the"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " ℓ-adic Galois representation, and the motivic Galois group itself. The conjectures predict these"
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": " should be compatible—the motivic Galois group should equal the Mumford-Tate group and its "
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "ℓ-adic extension should match the monodromy group."
    }
}
{
    "type": "content_block_delta",
    "index": 0,
    "delta": {
        "type": "thinking_delta",
        "thinking": "\n\nNow"
    }
}
{
